<p dir=3D"ltr"><strong>1. Adding the HTML page to your Git repo</strong></p=
>
<p dir=3D"ltr">The HTML file is the artifact you just published. To get it =
into your repo:</p>
<ol dir=3D"ltr">
<li>Open the artifact link, then use your browser's <strong>Save Page As</s=
trong> (Ctrl+S / Cmd+S) to download the <code>index.html</code></li>
<li>Drop it into your repo =E2=80=94 typically at the root or in a <code>do=
cs/</code> folder</li>
<li>Commit and push it</li>
</ol>
<p dir=3D"ltr">To serve it as a live GitHub Pages site:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li>Go to your repo =E2=86=92 <strong>Settings =E2=86=92 Pages</strong></li=
>
<li>Set source to your branch and folder (<code>/root</code> or <code>/docs=
</code>)</li>
<li>GitHub will give you a public URL like <code>https://yourusername.githu=
b.io/your-repo</code></li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"ltr"><strong>2. Adding to your GitHub profile project list</stron=
g></p>
<p dir=3D"ltr">There are two ways people do this:</p>
<ul dir=3D"ltr">
<li><strong>Pin the repo</strong> =E2=80=94 go to your GitHub profile, clic=
k <strong>Customize your pins</strong>, and select the repo. It shows up as=
 a card on your profile with description, language, and stars.</li>
<li><strong>Profile README</strong> =E2=80=94 if you have a repo named exac=
tly <code>yourusername/yourusername</code>, its <code>README.md</code> rend=
ers on your profile page. You can add a projects section there linking to t=
his repo by name.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<p dir=3D"ltr">Would you like me to generate a polished <code>README.md</co=
de> for the repo itself, or draft a profile README projects section you cou=
